# English Rewrite

---

Three hours ago, I got off work, came home, and went online, where I saw people mourning Yu Ying-shih. At the time, all I knew was that he was the idol of the famous blogger Professor Wang Wai-him; other than that, I knew nothing about his life.

But in the 21st century, you don't need to consult teachers for everything—with Google, you can learn about the whole world. After doing some research, I came to understand more and more why he was so famous, and why Professor Wang so loved to bring up Master Yu...

Master Yu was a professor at three Ivy League schools! How impressive is that! Just dropping that credential alone would scare people to death!

Like a stalker, I kept digging through Yu Ying-shih's background: what the Chinese Wikipedia said about him, what the English Wikipedia said, how people flattered him when he won his awards, and what today's obituaries say about him. A person I most respect surfaced in my mind... Teacher Yun-gen. They really are quite similar!

Unfortunately, Teacher Gen doesn't have a Harvard professorship, but their traditional Chinese mastery—erudite in the ancient and the modern, embodying "I am Huaxia"—is surely cut from the same cloth. Teacher Gen favors Daoism and Buddhism; Master Yu seems to be a Confucian fan.

But that's where the trouble begins.

Confucianism emphasizes benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and trustworthiness; hierarchy between elder and younger, ruler and subject. But if you studied at a top American university and got your PhD right in the heyday of the hippie era, could you really go around teaching people to "respect your teachers and honor the Way" and not engage in so much individualism? You'd end up begging on the streets.

Now, how a scholar avoids begging on the streets is knowledge that Confucius purchased with a lifetime of bitterness—his disciples could not possibly not know it. In old Master Kong's era, chaos was about to engulf the world. He traveled from state to state hoping to secure an official post and accomplish great things. But all he knew how to do was recite the rites of the Duke of Zhou, and he didn't know how to cater to the boss's needs. In the end, he returned empty-handed. However, failure is the mother of success—his students fared much better. The Grand Historian said: "The seventy disciples scattered to serve the various lords; the greatest became tutors, ministers, and chancellors; the lesser ones befriended and taught the scholar-officials." Why?

Because after several generations of transmission, they learned to read the rulers' faces. By Mencius's time, his interactions with rulers were already somewhat different—chatting and joking, knowing how to flatter the boss: "King of Qi, I see you can't even bear to kill an ox—you must be a benevolent ruler! Practice the kingly way, you've got what it takes!"

By the late Warring States period, those "Confucian" strategists had mastered the game of "ruler interviews" to perfection. To become officials, they threw away "benevolence" and "propriety" entirely, and everyone went off to study "Legalism," the new technology. That's why Xunzi, as a Confucian disciple, produced students who could become high officials in Qin Shi Huang's government. In Li Si, you certainly can't smell a trace of old Master Kong.

Today, with a hundred-plus countries in the United Nations, the situation is even more complicated than in the Spring and Autumn period. When a Confucian heir leaves China—that (ancient) land of rites and propriety—for the barbarian land of American military hegemony, is he really going to pull out the *Analects* and tell people to observe three years of mourning? Everyone knows how badly it went when old Master Kong and his students got doors slammed in their faces. So, in America, a disciple of Confucius naturally says the Master supported democracy—democracy is the great way, the Communist Party can go to hell—only then can you land an Ivy League professorship!

Some might ask: doesn't talking nonsense have consequences? Of course not! Confucius and Mencius have been dead for over two thousand years, and the Confucian tradition was smashed to pieces during the May Fourth Movement. As an academic authority at a top university in the sole superpower of the postwar era—even if you claimed Johnny Kung taught drug use and womanizing, people might believe you. After all, America's "theoretical nukes" are the real deal. The consequences of the Japanese not believing in such things—everyone saw that clearly back then.

So why could Master Yu, back then, casually translate a few passages from the *Book of Han* into English and get a PhD, with several universities inviting him to be a professor? *You know why.* They push you up onto the altar as an authority precisely so you can say with a straight face: "All of you here are losers. You think Confucius's thought is feudal? Oh dear, what a big misunderstanding! He was actually a champion of democracy! You don't have a Harvard professorship, so sit down and listen obediently!"

As for whether Confucian thought conflicts with democracy? Honestly, what would "sinologists" know about that? You say: the Republic of China is the example. Hey, buddy—is Taiwan's political system actually "Confucian"? The KMT crowd curses Tsai Ing-wen every single day—is that how Confucianism works? Does Tsai Ing-wen put on a big show of worshipping heaven and earth? No, right? Scientific spirit, no charlatanism—that violates Confucian principles. You think using Chinese characters makes you Confucian? You think Confucianism can only exist in China?

As I said before: Japan is Confucian, Britain is Confucian (at least among advanced nations, they're the most Confucian-like): the monarch emphasizes ritual and pomp, power is granted by heaven, supreme and unchallengeable, everyone in their layered hierarchy with no jumping ranks—only then is society stable. As for the successes and failures of their democratic systems, that's not something a Chinese-American has any standing to comment on.

---

# Commentary

**The main point, stripped of its sarcasm:**

The author is making a serious argument about the **instrumentalization and distortion of Confucianism by Western academia**, specifically by Chinese-born scholars who built careers in America. The core claims are:

1. **Confucianism, authentically understood, is hierarchical, ritualistic, and anti-egalitarian**—emphasizing deference, order, and the subordination of individual to social role. It is fundamentally at odds with liberal democracy and individualism.

2. **Scholars like Yu Ying-shih succeeded in the West by repackaging Confucianism to fit Western liberal values**—portraying Confucius as a proto-democrat or champion of freedom. The author sees this as intellectual opportunism: saying what the host culture wants to hear in exchange for prestige and position.

3. **This is not new—it's a survival strategy as old as Confucianism itself.** The author traces a lineage: Confucius himself failed to sell his rigid doctrine to rulers; his disciples learned to adapt; by Mencius's era they were flattering kings; by the Warring States period, "Confucians" had abandoned Confucian substance entirely (Xunzi's students becoming Legalists serving Qin). Adaptation-to-power is presented as the tradition's real, unspoken core skill.

4. **Western academic authority functions as an unassailable credential.** Because American universities held postwar intellectual hegemony, a Harvard professorship laundered any claim into truth—claims about Chinese history that a scholar could never make with a straight face in a Chinese context.

5. **The author's counter-examples (Japan, Britain) reveal his actual view of Confucianism:** it is a system of ritual hierarchy, divine-right authority, and fixed social order—the very opposite of democracy. Taiwan's democracy, in his telling, succeeded precisely by *abandoning* Confucian practice, not fulfilling it.

**In short:** the essay argues that Yu Ying-shih and similar émigré scholars were not genuine interpreters of Confucianism but performers of a Westernized caricature of it, rewarded for telling American academia that Chinese tradition was always secretly liberal. The author believes this flatters Western prejudice, misrepresents the tradition, and that such scholars lack the standing to pronounce on the compatibility of Confucianism and democracy at all.

**A note on the essay's weaknesses:** the argument relies heavily on ad hominem insinuation (that Yu's scholarship was shallow, that his PhD was granted for trivial work), guilt by association, and a romanticized, monolithic definition of "Confucianism" that many scholars would contest. The claim that Yu translated "a few passages of the *Book of Han*" to earn his doctorate is a caricature of his actual dissertation on Han intellectual history. The essay is more effective as an expression of resentment toward Western academic gatekeeping and diaspora intellectual fashion than as a rigorous critique. [end of text]


